WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.600
The database is only half
of the authentication path.

2
00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:07.457
An application that moved its
user sign-in to Entra ID but

3
00:00:07.457 --> 00:00:11.313
still reaches SQL Server with
a SQL login hasn't finished.

4
00:00:11.313 --> 00:00:14.591
The chain runs from user to
application to database,

5
00:00:14.591 --> 00:00:17.412
and both legs have to
leave Active Directory.

6
00:00:17.412 --> 00:00:22.856
The program's LDAP-dependent-application
plan sorts applications into four classes,

7
00:00:22.856 --> 00:00:27.013
in strict preference order; the
numbers are identifiers, not ranks.

8
00:00:27.013 --> 00:00:32.277
Class 3, OpenID Connect, is the
destination: Entra ID issues the tokens,

9
00:00:32.277 --> 00:00:34.956
there's no LDAP bind and no AD query,

10
00:00:34.956 --> 00:00:40.037
and the application reaches SQL
Server with its managed identity.

11
00:00:40.037 --> 00:00:45.497
Class 2, SAML, removes the LDAP
dependency but ranks below OIDC,

12
00:00:45.497 --> 00:00:50.338
because Microsoft recommends
OIDC for new development.

13
00:00:50.338 --> 00:00:54.773
Class 1, Application Proxy, fronts
an application that can't change;

14
00:00:54.773 --> 00:00:58.968
Entra preauthenticates users,
so Conditional Access applies,

15
00:00:58.968 --> 00:01:03.162
but the application's own AD
dependency stays behind the proxy.

16
00:01:03.162 --> 00:01:07.407
Class 4, Entra Domain
Services, is the last resort:

17
00:01:07.407 --> 00:01:11.438
a managed domain with
LDAP, Kerberos and NTLM.

18
00:01:11.438 --> 00:01:16.998
Its NTLM is still NTLM, and an instance
serving a Class 4 application can't

19
00:01:16.998 --> 00:01:22.651
reach the Entra-exclusive posture, so
it carries a sunset-dated exception.

20
00:01:22.651 --> 00:01:25.588
The class describes only the user axis.

21
00:01:25.588 --> 00:01:29.061
The database connection is a
second axis, set by evidence:

22
00:01:29.061 --> 00:01:32.017
does the deployed driver
support the Entra mode,

23
00:01:32.017 --> 00:01:36.598
can the application use a managed
identity or must its code refresh tokens,

24
00:01:36.598 --> 00:01:40.810
does pooling survive per-identity
tokens, does the vendor support it,

25
00:01:40.810 --> 00:01:43.913
and does the platform have
an Entra-capable driver.

26
00:01:43.913 --> 00:01:49.539
That places each application in
column A, configuration only;

27
00:01:49.539 --> 00:01:54.062
B, code change; C, vendor-dependent;
or D, no Entra path.

28
00:01:54.062 --> 00:01:58.165
OIDC or SAML in column A
moves now; B is scheduled;

29
00:01:58.165 --> 00:02:02.987
C gets a dated exception; D
means exception or retirement.

30
00:02:02.987 --> 00:02:06.995
The application audit supplies the
user axis, and it has to be joined

31
00:02:06.995 --> 00:02:11.140
to the authentication audit before
an instance shows its full chain.
